metadata.rules_text:
{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last ~5–10 days show an upswing (higher highs / positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path; the bias grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger beyond ~day 10).",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts often follow the prior range and react too slowly to breakouts; if price has recently broken above the 20–30d range/MA, steepen the forecast slope upward rather than keeping it flat.",
    "Underestimation of post-dip rebound: after a sharp drop in history, the model rebounds too cautiously; apply faster mean reversion upward in the first 1–2 weeks when the last observed price is below the recent average.",
    "Horizon-specific compression: mid-horizon (≈days 10–25) forecasts are too range-bound versus outcomes; widen the forecast distribution upward in bullish regimes (i.e., allow higher highs).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk after strong rallies: when the model already projects a sharp late rise, outcomes can revert/flatten; dampen extreme late-horizon moves (days 25–30) unless the recent trend is still accelerating."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 8 onward; apply an upward level shift (larger for mid/late horizon).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market transitions into an upswing (as in the July rally), the model lags and fails to capture the slope; add a positive momentum term that increases with horizon.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small/mixed, but mid-horizon (days ~6–20) underprediction grows and late horizon (days ~20–30) can be materially low versus outcomes; use a bias curve that ramps up with lead time.",
    "Spike handling asymmetry: after upward shocks, the model either smooths them away or predicts only partial continuation; increase sensitivity to upside breakouts (e.g., if last 3–5 days are rising, lift the whole path).",
    "Late-horizon mean-reversion miscalibration: the model often mean-reverts toward ~68–70 while realized prices stay elevated (~71–73); reduce mean-reversion strength when recent volatility/momentum is high."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the recent history shows an upswing (e.g., multiple higher closes / breakout), the model under-forecasts the level over the next ~5–20 days. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger in days ~7–20).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to sharp upside moves; it predicts a flatter path while realized prices keep trending up. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope to lift the mid-horizon trajectory.",
    "Underestimation of peak magnitude after upside shocks: around spike events, the model captures direction but not amplitude (realized peaks exceed forecast). Inflate forecast variance/peaks when recent daily range/volatility is elevated.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after regime change: when the true market turns down after a run-up, the model can keep extrapolating the prior rise and ends up too high in the last ~5–10 forecast days. Introduce mean-reversion damping beyond ~day 20, especially if history shows stretched levels vs recent average.",
    "Horizon asymmetry: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller and often slightly low; mid-horizon (days 6–20) shows the largest negative bias in uptrends; far-horizon (days 21–30) can flip to positive bias if a reversal occurs. Use regime detection (trend vs reversal) to switch from momentum-boost (mid) to mean-reversion (far)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing (higher highs / positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path (≈ +1.5 to +3.0 €/t), strongest in days 5–20 where underprediction is largest.",
    "Trend/volatility underreaction: the model smooths moves and misses rally amplitude; increase forecast sensitivity to recent momentum (e.g., scale recent 10-day return contribution up) so peaks are higher and arrive sooner.",
    "Lag around spikes: after a sharp up-move in history, the model’s peak occurs later and lower than realized; pull forward the peak timing by a few days and lift the near-term (days 1–10) by ~+0.5 to +2.0 €/t when momentum is strong.",
    "Late-horizon mean-reversion error: the model often stays too high at the far end (days ~25–30) when the realized path rolls over; apply stronger downward mean reversion in the tail (reduce days 25–30 by ~1–3 €/t) when recent volatility is elevated or when the model projects a continued rise into the tail.",
    "Asymmetry: corrections should be regime-dependent—apply larger positive corrections during sustained rallies, but allow faster negative adjustment in the last third of the horizon after extended run-ups (to avoid overestimating persistence)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing/acceleration, add an upward bias to the forecast path (model is ~1–3 EUR too low for much of the first ~15–20 days).",
    "Trend/level lag: the forecast reacts too slowly to regime shifts (especially rallies). Heuristic: if spot is above the 20–30d mean and the mean is rising, shift the whole forecast level up (reduce smoothing/lag).",
    "Peak/volatility underestimation: the model misses sharp upside spikes (e.g., true jumps to 72–73 while forecast stays ~69–71). Heuristic: increase amplitude during high momentum/large prior daily moves (widen forecast range upward).",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: beyond ~day 20, the model tends to drift too low vs realized levels (except when a genuine selloff occurs). Heuristic: dampen late-horizon downward drift; pull tail forecasts toward the recent mean unless strong negative momentum is present.",
    "Selloff timing mismatch: when a real late drop happens (e.g., true falls to ~65), the model often predicts it earlier/higher and then fails to match the exact timing. Heuristic: delay mean-reversion/selloff calls unless confirmed by consecutive down days; otherwise keep forecasts closer to current level."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underprediction (positive bias needed): When the recent 1–2 week slope is positive, the model systematically undershoots the next ~5–20 trading days by roughly +1 to +3 €/t (largest around days 7–15). Apply an upward level shift proportional to recent momentum.",
    "Turning-point lag: The model reacts late to regime changes. After a breakout to new highs, it keeps forecasting near the prior range for ~1 week; after the peak, it keeps forecasting elevated for too long. Add a momentum/acceleration term to shorten lag (boost early in breakouts; cut faster after peaks).",
    "Post-spike overshoot on long horizon: Following a sharp rise, the model’s far-horizon path stays too high versus realized outcomes (it fails to price the subsequent drawdown). From ~day 20 onward after a spike, apply stronger mean-reversion (downward tilt) rather than maintaining elevated levels.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias pattern: Near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (days 6–15) tends to be too low in rising markets; long-horizon (days 20–30) tends to be too high after peaks. Use piecewise corrections by horizon bucket (1–5, 6–15, 16–30).",
    "Amplitude damping: Forecast volatility is too muted during rallies (misses magnitude of upside) but too persistent after the top (doesn’t damp quickly enough). Increase response to positive shocks early, but increase decay rate of shocks over time (faster fade-out)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~10–15 days. Apply a negative level shift (downward correction) that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after a run-up, the model keeps projecting elevated/flat prices while the market mean-reverts lower. Add a regime rule: if last 5–10 days show a sharp rise, force a downward drift in the 2–4 week horizon.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when spot jumps into the low-70s, the model extrapolates further gains (often into 72–74) but outcomes peak sooner and then fall. Cap upside projections following spikes and increase probability/size of pullbacks.",
    "Underestimates near-term continuation during early rally phase: in the first ~1–7 days after a breakout, forecasts are slightly below realized (misses immediate momentum). Add a small short-horizon upward adjustment when recent returns are strongly positive.",
    "Horizon-dependent error shape: near-term errors are smaller and sometimes negative (under-forecast), while long-horizon errors are large and positive (over-forecast). Use a piecewise correction: slight +adj for days 1–7 in strong up-momentum regimes; progressively larger −adj for days 10–30, especially after a rally.",
    "Mean-reversion strength under-modeled: realized paths revert toward mid/high-60s faster than forecast implies. Increase mean-reversion speed parameter (or pull forecasts toward a rolling 20–30d mean) when price is >~2–3 EUR above that mean."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime bias: the model is too low during short bullish bursts (first ~5–15 days in Examples 1–3), then becomes too high when the market turns bearish (Examples 2–5). Apply a regime-dependent bias: add +1 to +2 €/t when recent momentum is positive; subtract −3 to −8 €/t when recent momentum turns negative.",
    "Turning-point lag: forecasts react slowly to reversals. When the last 3–5 days show a clear direction change, pull the whole path toward the new direction (increase slope in upturns; decrease slope in downturns) rather than keeping the prior trend.",
    "Downturn underestimation at longer horizons: beyond ~day 15–30, the model systematically fails to capture drawdowns and stays anchored near ~70–74 while realized prices fall to mid-60s/low-60s (Examples 2–5). Add an extra negative drift for horizons >15 days when recent volatility rises and momentum is negative.",
    "Spike handling: after upward spikes, the model mean-reverts too quickly and underpredicts the persistence of elevated levels (Examples 1–3 around the 72–73 area). Reduce mean-reversion strength for ~7–10 days after a spike (keep levels higher).",
    "Crash handling: after the market starts selling off, the model overshoots upward (predicts new highs) instead of reverting down (Examples 4–5). If price breaks below a recent support/short MA, cap forecast highs and enforce faster mean reversion downward.",
    "Level anchoring: forecasts cluster around ~70 regardless of realized trend (especially in Examples 2–5). Increase sensitivity to recent returns: widen forecast distribution and allow the level to migrate (down to mid-60s) when the last 10–15 days’ average is falling."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic positive bias: forecasts are generally too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~2 weeks. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Missed downside regime shift: when history shows a local peak/spike (e.g., ~70–71) the model extrapolates strength, but outcomes often mean-revert and then sell off. Add a mean-reversion penalty after spikes (reduce projected path rather than keeping it elevated).",
    "Lag in turning points: forecasts react too slowly to emerging declines; realized series rolls over earlier and faster. Introduce a momentum/acceleration check: if last 5–10 days show weakening, pull forward the downturn in the forecast.",
    "Overshoot persistence error: model keeps prices clustered around ~70–73 even when realized distribution shifts down into mid-60s. Add a regime-dependent cap: if recent volatility increases and downside breaks occur, compress the upper tail and allow lower levels sooner.",
    "Horizon shape error: near-term (first week) errors are modest, but mid/long horizon errors become increasingly optimistic and too smooth. Increase forecast variance and downward skew with horizon during post-peak conditions."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are too low in the first ~10–15 days during upswings (+~1 to +3 €/t typical), but become too high beyond ~day 15–30 when the market turns down (often +~4 to +10 €/t by the end). Apply a bias that shifts from + (early) to − (late) depending on recent trend.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts late to regime changes. After a local peak, it keeps projecting elevated/flat prices instead of the observed sharp drawdown. Add a ‘trend-break’ detector (e.g., if recent momentum weakens) and force earlier downward adjustment.",
    "Oversmoothing/underestimating volatility: true outcomes show abrupt drops; forecasts remain clustered around 70–73. Increase downside volatility allowance when recent range expands; widen bands and pull the mean lower when downside tail risk rises.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts toward a high level (~71–72) even when history starts rolling over. Anchor mean reversion to a shorter-term mean (e.g., 5–10d) when momentum is negative, rather than a fixed long-run level.",
    "Spike handling: after short-lived rallies/spikes, the model extrapolates the higher level too long. Use a spike-decay rule: if a spike occurs without follow-through (next 2–3 days not making new highs), decay forecasts back toward pre-spike levels quickly (within ~5–10 days)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-change / mean-reversion miss: forecasts stay anchored near ~70–73 even when the market transitions into a sustained downtrend; apply a negative drift adjustment when recent returns turn negative for several consecutive days.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–10) is slightly low during the final leg of an upswing, but medium/long horizon (days ~15–30) becomes strongly high-biased as the realized path mean-reverts and sells off; increase mean-reversion strength with horizon.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: after a run-up to ~72–73, the model extrapolates elevated levels instead of anticipating pullbacks; cap forward levels relative to recent peak and force partial retracement (e.g., fade a portion of the last 5–10 day rise).",
    "Lag in turning points: once the true series starts falling, forecasts decline too slowly and remain too smooth; add a momentum-triggered slope change (steepen downward path) when price breaks below a short moving average / recent support.",
    "Volatility underestimation on the downside: realized outcomes show sharper late-horizon drops (to low/mid-60s) than forecasts; widen downside scenarios or apply asymmetric error correction (larger adjustments for negative shocks than positive)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~3–8 EUR), especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model extrapolates recent strength and misses regime shifts. When the last 5–10 days show weakening/rolling over, reduce the entire forward path (don’t project the prior uptrend).",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following a spike to ~71–73, the model keeps prices elevated, but outcomes mean-revert and then sell off. Add explicit mean-reversion toward the 20–30d average and cap upside persistence after spikes.",
    "Horizon-specific error: near-term (days 1–7) errors are smaller/mixed; mid-to-long horizon (days 8–30) errors become strongly upward. Use a horizon-weighted correction (small adjustment early, larger negative adjustment later).",
    "Volatility asymmetry: model underestimates downside tail risk (sharp drops), producing overly smooth, high paths. Introduce a downside-skew adjustment when recent momentum turns negative (e.g., if 3–5d return < 0, widen downside and lower the mean forecast)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~10 trading days (often +5 to +12 EUR/t by days 15–30). Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Regime-change blindness / delayed downturn recognition: when the recent history contains a local peak/spike (e.g., ~71–73), the model extrapolates strength, but the market mean-reverts and then sells off. Add a mean-reversion term that pulls forecasts toward the 30d trailing mean and strengthens after sharp up-moves.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following short-term rallies, the model predicts continued grind higher (72–74), while outcomes roll over quickly (down to low/mid-60s). Cap upside continuation: if last 5–10d momentum is strongly positive, dampen forecast slope and reduce levels for horizons >7d.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon errors widen materially. Use piecewise correction: minimal adjustment for days 1–5, moderate for days 6–10, strong downward + mean-reversion for days 11–30.",
    "Underestimation of downside volatility: realized paths show sharper drops than forecast. Introduce asymmetric adjustment: when recent volatility is elevated or price is above its 20–30d average, skew corrections downward more than upward.",
    "Trend persistence overestimated: model maintains a high plateau (around 71–73) even as realized prices transition to a lower range (mid-60s). Add a 'range-shift' heuristic: if price is > (30d mean + 1σ), pull forecasts back toward mean by an increasing fraction with horizon (e.g., 30% at 10d, 60% at 30d)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive bias beyond ~10–15 day horizon: model stays in 70–74 range while realized prices trend down into low/mid-60s. Apply a horizon-increasing downward bias correction (small in days 1–7, large in days 15–30).",
    "Missed regime shift / mean reversion after local highs: when recent history includes a spike to ~71–73, the model extrapolates strength; realized path mean-reverts and often sells off. After any 2–3 day upswing or print >~72, damp the forecast slope and pull levels back toward the 20–30d mean.",
    "Overshoot persistence error: model forecasts are too smooth and too persistent (keeps elevated plateau), while outcomes show a sharp step-down around ~day 16–22. Introduce a decay term so deviations from the recent mean shrink faster with horizon.",
    "Downside tail underestimation: model rarely projects prices <~69 even when realized drops to ~63–66. Add a conditional lower-tail adjustment: if momentum is negative or volatility rises, widen downside and shift distribution lower for days 10–30.",
    "Turning-point lag: model reacts late to emerging downtrends (keeps rising/flat forecasts even as realized begins falling). Use recent 5–10d momentum as a sign filter—if momentum ≤ 0, cap any upward drift in the forecast and bias next 2–4 weeks downward."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: after a local peak/spike in history, the model extrapolates strength, but the market mean-reverts and often sells off. Add a regime rule: if last 5–10 days show an upswing/spike, dampen the forecast slope and pull levels back toward the 20–30d mean.",
    "Overshoot on declines: when the true series enters a sustained downtrend, the model reacts too slowly and stays near the prior range (≈70–74) while outcomes fall into mid-60s. Introduce faster downward adjustment: if recent momentum is negative, subtract an extra penalty that grows with cumulative negative return.",
    "Horizon-shape error: forecasts are too flat/high at the back end (days 15–30) and fail to capture large drawdowns. Enforce stronger mean reversion at longer horizons by blending progressively more weight to long-run mean / recent realized trend rather than the model path.",
    "Volatility underestimation in direction: model keeps prices within a narrow high band while realized outcomes exhibit larger directional moves downward. Add a directional volatility scaler: expand downside moves more than upside moves when macro/trend regime is bearish."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–12 EUR), especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Missed regime shift / mean reversion: after the market turns down, the model keeps projecting stable-to-rising prices in the low/mid-70s while outcomes mean-revert and trend down into the mid-60s. Add a mean-reversion term toward the recent 10–20d average and reduce trend persistence.",
    "Lag in trend changes: model reacts too slowly to emerging declines; it extrapolates prior strength. Use recent slope (last 5–10d) as an override—if slope is negative, force forecast path downward rather than flat/up.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: when history contains a spike to ~72–73, the model projects continued upside (73–75) but outcomes reverse sharply. Cap upside continuation after a local max and impose post-spike pullback (e.g., subtract extra 1–2 EUR for days 5–15 following a spike).",
    "Horizon-specific error profile: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors grow materially and stay one-sided (too high). Apply a horizon-weighted correction (small cut near-term, larger cut long-term)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model reacts too slowly to bullish reversals; after a local bottom it underestimates the subsequent rebound. Add a momentum term when the last 5–10 days show rising prices.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors grow with forecast horizon (week 3–4 most biased). Use a ramping correction that increases with lead time (e.g., linear increase in uplift by day).",
    "Missed upside volatility: the model dampens rallies and fails to capture late-horizon spikes (e.g., days ~20–30). Inflate positive moves when recent realized volatility is elevated.",
    "Asymmetry: downside is captured reasonably in the first few days, but upside is consistently undercalled thereafter. Use asymmetric correction (stronger uplift when forecast path is flat/down but recent realized path is up)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market transitions into an upswing (as in these samples), the model lags and misses the slope. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 days’ return; scale it up for horizons >10 days.",
    "Late-horizon catch-up failure: errors widen materially in the back half of the 30d window (often +2 to +6 €/t underprediction). Use a horizon-weighted correction (e.g., linear ramp) rather than a flat offset.",
    "Spike/rally underestimation: during sharp rallies the model predicts partial mean reversion too early and caps highs. If recent volatility/positive returns exceed a threshold, reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels.",
    "Shape mismatch (too flat): forecast paths are smoother than outcomes; realized series shows larger swings and higher peaks. Increase forecast variance conditional on recent realized volatility (volatility scaling)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underprediction (horizon > ~15d): when the recent 30d history shows a clear down-then-up turn (local trough followed by higher highs), the model systematically underestimates the subsequent rally; apply an upward drift add-on that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Late-horizon level bias: forecasts for days ~20–30 are too low by several €/t in rising regimes; use a horizon-dependent bias correction (e.g., +0 to +1 early, ramping to +3 to +7 by day 30 when momentum is positive).",
    "Lag/slow reaction to acceleration: the model captures direction early but misses the speed of the move (true series steepens while forecast stays flat); if the last ~5–10 realized days show accelerating gains, steepen the forecast slope (increase daily increments) rather than shifting the whole path uniformly.",
    "Post-breakout underestimation: after prices break above the recent 30d range (new local highs), the model mean-reverts too aggressively; reduce mean-reversion strength and allow persistence (keep forecasts closer to the breakout level, especially days 10–30).",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are smoother than realized outcomes, missing late spikes; widen the forecast distribution / add upside convexity in bullish regimes (e.g., add a positive skew adjustment rather than symmetric noise)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market turns up, the model follows with insufficient slope (lags the upswing). Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 days’ return to steepen the path during rising regimes.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially. Use a bias ramp that increases with forecast horizon.",
    "Missed breakout/rally tails: large positive outcomes (late-horizon rallies to high 60s) are not captured; cap the model’s mean-reversion strength when recent volatility is rising, allowing higher terminal levels.",
    "After dips, rebound is underestimated: following local lows in history, the model predicts only modest recovery while realized prices rebound strongly. If last observed price is below a short MA (e.g., 10d) and MA slope turns positive, boost weeks 2–4 forecasts."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias in errors: model forecasts are systematically too low versus realized prices, especially from ~day 15 onward. Apply a positive level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 3–4).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the recent history shows a turn from decline to rebound, the model lags the upswing by ~3–7 days. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope/return to pull the path upward earlier.",
    "Late-horizon catch-up missing: true outcomes exhibit sustained rallies into days ~20–30, while the model stays range-bound/mean-reverting. Reduce mean-reversion strength beyond ~2 weeks; allow drift to persist when recent volatility + positive slope are present.",
    "Convexity error after breakouts: once prices break above the recent 30d range, realized prices continue trending higher; the model caps/plateaus. Add a breakout rule: if spot > 20–30d moving average and > prior 10d high, uplift weeks 3–4 forecasts by an additional premium.",
    "Horizon-specific correction heuristic: +0 to +0.5 EUR (days 1–7), +1 to +2 EUR (days 8–14), +3 to +5 EUR (days 15–30), scaled by recent 10d momentum (e.g., multiply by 1 + k*momentum)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/rally, the model systematically under-forecasts the next 2–4 weeks (misses sustained upside). Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent negative bias: errors are modest early but become increasingly negative beyond ~day 10; treat this as a drift/level bias rather than random noise (add a positive ramp to forecasts after day 10).",
    "Lag in regime change: after a local low and subsequent breakout, forecasts behave as if mean reversion will dominate and react too slowly to the new higher regime. Use a momentum/regime filter: if price is above the 10–15d moving average and MA slope is positive, reduce mean-reversion strength and lift the whole path.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth and too low-amplitude versus realized moves, especially during rallies. Inflate forecast variance (wider swings) and allow higher highs in the outer horizon.",
    "Late-horizon ceiling effect: when spot is in mid-60s and momentum is positive, the model tends to cap outcomes in the mid/high-60s while realized prints reach ~68–70. Add a conditional cap-lift: if recent high is rising, raise the upper band/target by ~2–4 EUR for days 15–30."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: model systematically underestimates the level, especially from ~day 10 onward; apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: during sustained rallies the model reacts too slowly (forecasts stay near low/mid-60s while realized moves into high-60s); add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope to lift the whole path.",
    "Underestimation grows with horizon: errors are modest early but widen materially by weeks 3–4; use horizon-dependent scaling (e.g., add a linear ramp or multiply deviations from last price by >1 for days 15–30).",
    "Peaks are capped too low: the model misses upside spikes/upper tail (realized highs ~69–70 vs forecasts ~66–68); increase volatility/upper-quantile allowance when recent realized volatility is elevated.",
    "Mean reversion too strong: forecasts revert back toward ~63–66 even when the regime has shifted upward; reduce mean-reversion strength when price breaks above recent 20–30d range or when consecutive higher closes occur."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward when the market trends up. Apply an upward level shift (increasing with horizon).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when history transitions from a dip to a rebound, the model follows but with insufficient slope; it misses sustained rallies. Add a momentum/trend term that increases forecasts when the last 5–10 days show positive drift.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small/mixed, but medium-to-long horizon (days ~10–30) errors become strongly negative. Use a correction that ramps up with forecast step (e.g., linear or convex uplift by lead time).",
    "Lag after regime change: the model behaves as if mean-reverting to the recent range, while outcomes break to a higher regime (mid/high 60s). Detect breakouts (price > recent 10–20d band) and reduce mean-reversion strength / shift the anchor upward.",
    "Amplitude compression: forecast volatility is too low versus outcomes during rallies (peaks capped ~66–67 while realized reaches ~69–70). Increase forecast variance/upper tail when trend is positive and recent realized volatility is rising."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising markets: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing (positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path. In these samples the model is ~2–6 EUR too low on average, with the bias increasing with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: apply a correction that grows with lead time (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–30). The model’s error is modest early but becomes large in weeks 2–4 as it fails to follow the sustained rally.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when price breaks above the recent 30d range or posts consecutive higher highs, increase forecast drift (daily increments) rather than keeping a flat/mean-reverting profile; the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts.",
    "Over-mean-reversion: reduce pullback strength after dips during an emerging uptrend. The model repeatedly projects returns toward ~62–65 while realized prices re-rate to ~67–70.",
    "Volatility compression: widen forecast amplitude after a breakout. The model’s path is too smooth and misses the magnitude of the move; scale deviations from the starting level upward (especially mid/late horizon) when recent realized volatility rises."
  ]
}

{
